course: world
lesson: 38
title: Sleep, exercise and growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sleep is not wasted time. While you sleep the body releases growth hormone and repairs itself, and the brain sorts and stores what you learned during the day.
explain: Children of 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night. Bright screens late in the evening make it harder to fall asleep, because the brain takes the light as a sign that it is still day.
explain: Exercise makes muscles stronger, including the heart, and makes bones thicker and stronger. About an hour of active play a day is good for children.
explain: A muscle that is used grows stronger; a muscle that is not used gets weaker. Astronauts in space, where they do not have to hold up their weight, lose muscle and bone unless they exercise every day.
explain: People grow fastest as babies and again in their teenage years, at puberty. Growing depends on genes, food, sleep and exercise; everyone grows at their own pace.
fact: During sleep the body releases growth hormone and repairs itself.
fact: Sleep helps the brain to remember what it learned.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night.
fact: Bright screen light at night makes it harder to fall asleep.
fact: Exercise makes muscles, the heart and bones stronger.
fact: Children should be active for about an hour every day.
fact: Muscles that are not used become weaker.
fact: Astronauts exercise every day in space to keep their muscles and bones strong.
fact: Children grow fastest as babies and again during puberty.
fact: A warm-up before sport and a cool-down after help prevent injuries.
fact: Everyone grows at their own pace.
exercise: What happens in the body during sleep? | It repairs itself and releases growth hormone | It stops working completely | It forgets everything learned that day | It stops breathing for hours
exercise: About how much sleep does a 10-year-old need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 3 to 4 hours | About 5 to 6 hours | About 16 to 18 hours
exercise: How does sleep help learning? | The brain stores what it learned during the day | The brain empties itself of all memories so it can start again | Dreams teach us new school subjects | It does not help learning at all
exercise: Why does a bright screen at bedtime make sleep harder? | The brain takes the light as a sign it is still day | Screens make the room too cold | Screens use up all the oxygen the body needs to fall asleep | Screens make the eyes too heavy
exercise: What does exercise do to the heart? | It makes the heart muscle stronger | It makes the heart smaller and weaker | It stops the heart for a while | It has no effect on the heart
exercise: How long should children be active each day? | About an hour | About a minute | About ten hours | Only once a week
exercise: What happens to muscles that are not used? | They become weaker | They become stronger | They turn into bone | They stay exactly the same
exercise: Why do astronauts exercise every day in space? | Without weight to hold up, their muscles and bones weaken | Space air is too thick to breathe while resting | It keeps the spaceship moving | It stops them from growing too tall
exercise: When do children grow fastest after babyhood? | During puberty, in the teenage years | At age five exactly | Only while they are ill | In old age
exercise: Why do sportspeople warm up before a game? | To prepare the muscles and avoid injury | To make themselves sleepy before the match | To use up all their energy before the start | To make their bones softer and more bendy
exercise: Which helps a child grow well? | Good food, enough sleep and exercise | Staying up all night as often as possible | Eating only sweets and skipping proper meals | Never moving at all, to save up energy
exercise: What does exercise do to bones? | It makes them thicker and stronger | It makes them thinner and lighter to carry | It makes them bend like rubber | It makes them stop growing
exercise: Is it normal for two friends of the same age to be different heights? | Yes, everyone grows at their own pace | No, all children of one age are the same height | Only if one of them is ill | Only if one eats no food
test: Mia stayed up late playing games and slept only 6 hours before a test. What is most likely? | She will find it harder to think and remember | She will remember more than usual | Sleep has nothing at all to do with how well you do in tests | She will grow taller that day
test: A boy broke his leg and had it in a cast for six weeks. Afterwards that leg looks thinner. Why? | Its unused muscles got weaker | The cast squeezed the bone shorter | The other leg grew too fast meanwhile | The blood left that leg forever
test: Why would an astronaut back from six months in space find walking hard at first? | Their muscles and bones weakened without weight to carry | Earth has less gravity than space | Their legs got longer in space | They forgot how to walk in their brain
test: A girl wants a stronger heart for running. What should she do? | Run and play actively often, a little more each week | Rest all day to save the heart | Eat lots of sugar before every run | Run as fast as possible once a year
test: Which bedtime habit helps sleep most? | Putting screens away and keeping the room dark | Watching a bright tablet in bed | Drinking a big cola before bed | Going to bed at a new time every night of the week
test: Two runners: one warms up, one does not. Who is more likely to get hurt? | The one who did not warm up | The one who warmed up | Both are equally likely in every case | Neither, warming up is only for cold days
test: Which is true? | Both muscles and bones get stronger when they are used | Bones never change after birth | Exercise makes bones weaker | Only the muscles in the arms can get stronger, never bones or legs
test: A 12-year-old is shorter than his classmates. What is most likely? | He grows at his own pace and may catch up later | He will never grow again | He sleeps too much | He has the wrong number of bones
test: If a child slept only four hours a night for many weeks, what might happen? | Growth, learning and health could suffer | They would grow much faster | They would learn twice as fast | Nothing at all, because sleep is not really needed
test: why'd astronauts bother working out up in space | So their muscles and bones stay strong | So they stay warm in the freezing cold of space | So they float the right way up in the cabin | Because there is nothing else to do up there
test: phone in bed till late n then can't sleep, how come | Bright screen light makes it harder to fall asleep | Phones make the bed too warm | The phone uses up your sleep | Late nights make you grow too fast to sleep properly
test: my brother says sleep is just wasted time, nothing happens. true? | No, the body repairs itself and the brain stores what it learned | Yes, the body switches off fully | Yes, only dreams happen in sleep | No, sleep makes muscles weaker
test: Zoe reckons everyone the same age should be the same height. right? | No, everyone grows at their own pace | Yes, the same age means the same height | Yes, unless they eat sweets | No, older children are always shorter
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the functions of sleep. | The body repairs itself and the brain consolidates learning | The body ceases all activity until waking | Growth occurs solely during waking hours | Memory is erased during sleep
exam: State the quantity of sleep recommended nightly for children aged six to twelve years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately five to six hours | Approximately fifteen to eighteen hours | Approximately three to four hours
exam: Identify the principal reason for which astronauts exercise daily in space. | To preserve the strength of their muscles and bones | To generate warmth within the vessel during the night | To propel the vessel through space | To increase their weight
exam: Determine the consequence for a muscle that is not exercised. | It becomes weaker | It becomes stronger | It is unaltered | It becomes bone
